#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252 :topic/difficulty 0.6018997901213411 :topic/id #uuid "8c876864-a853-56d6-a887-91d7f8f1cf14" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087695 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087502 :question/id #uuid "9c82456c-49ea-5a5e-b0a2-0ab17059acee" :question/math-academy-id "e-4390" :question/problem "Which of the following are zero matrices?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$P=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "- $P$ is a zero matrix since *all* its entries are zeros.\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a zero matrix since some of its entries are not zeros (for instance, $q_{12}=0.1\\neq0$).\n\n- $R$ is a zero matrix since *all* its entries are zeros.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $R$ are zero matrices."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddb59990-7419-585f-aa1b-901d59c8cfc1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Zero Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087503 :question/id #uuid "bcb710d0-a687-5686-ae3a-0d0773857530" :question/math-academy-id "e-4391" :question/problem "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$U=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$Z=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "- $U$ is *not* a square matrix since the numbers of rows and columns are not the same. It has $2$ rows and $3$ columns, so it's a $2 \\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- $W$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $3$ rows and $3$ columns, so it is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- $Z$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $2$ rows and $2$ columns, so it is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, $W$ and $Z$ are square matrices."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca13445-0218-5950-967e-a68bcfec32c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087504 :question/id #uuid "79974103-2da9-599a-b33b-abac3d32613a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4392" :question/problem "Which of the following are diagonal matrices?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$S=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "- $Q$ is a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. Also, all the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nNotice that $Q$ is not an identity matrix since the diagonal entries are not all ones.\n\n- $R$ is *not* a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. But some of the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are not zeros (for instance, $r_{13}=1 \\neq 0$).\n\n- $S$ is a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. Also, all the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nNotice that $S$ is not an identity matrix since the diagonal entries are not all ones.\n\nTherefore, only $Q$ and $S$ are diagonal matrices."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09fa0899-a3d2-584f-8cdd-a471f8cfd16b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Special Square Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087698 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087505 :question/id #uuid "0e3ad3cd-f7c4-55f4-8a0b-b850283bc6b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4393" :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix?\n \n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$F=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $F$ is a matrix that has a single column. Therefore, it is a column vector."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ddadb-471a-5195-a0f5-fd44bdc8f780" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Other Matrices"}] :topic/math-academy-id 862 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :topic/title "Zero, Square, Diagonal and Identity Matrices"}]}
